.TH MINIOS-MODULE-MANAGER 1 "September 2026" "MiniOS Module Manager 1.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
minios-module-manager \- inspect, create and manage MiniOS modules
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B minios-module-manager
.RI [ module-file ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B MiniOS Module Manager
is a GTK 3 interface for MiniOS module composition and module creation.
It runs as the desktop user and delegates privileged or filesystem-sensitive
operations to MiniOS Tools through fixed argument vectors.
.PP
The application has two primary workspaces: Modules and Create.
The Modules workspace distinguishes the modules composing the running system
from those selected for the next boot.
.SH MODULES
.TP 4
.B Running Now
Shows the authoritative ordered runtime module composition reported by MiniOS
Tools. Runtime activation and deactivation are available only when the mounted
root union is AUFS.
.TP 4
.B Next Boot
Shows the authoritative ordered module composition selected by the current
MiniOS boot rules. User modules can be added to durable module storage when
MiniOS Tools reports that such storage is available. Excluding a user module
moves it to disabled storage, from which it can be included again. Permanent
deletion is available only for an already excluded removable module and
requires separate confirmation. These actions do not change Running Now.
.PP
Selecting a module opens read-only details. When its backing source file is
available, the details also show its SquashFS contents and allow extraction to
a new directory. Extraction is an explicit operation and never overwrites an
existing destination. Protected active-module backing files can be inspected
after administrator authentication. Extraction shows cancellable phase progress
and streamed backend diagnostics.
.SH CREATE
The Create workspace provides reviewed workflows for repository or local Debian
packages, installation scripts, an interactive chroot, existing folders, and
the current session changes.
.PP
Every workflow separates configuration, review, execution, and result. Creating
a module does not activate it or add it to the next boot automatically.
.PP
Package, installation-script, and interactive-chroot workflows select a build
base from the active stack. A numbered selection includes every active numbered
module through that level and every active unnumbered module. Package creation
does not install Recommends or Suggests unless each option is enabled explicitly;
missing local APT indexes can be refreshed before package-name completion is
used. Creation supports zstd, LZ4, XZ, LZO, and gzip where MiniOS Tools supports
the selected method.
.PP
The interactive chroot uses an embedded VTE terminal and the protected
.BR chroot2sb (1)
prepare/shell/finish/cancel lifecycle. Current-session capture uses cancellable
.BR savechanges (1)
and its standard MiniOS exclusion policy.
.SH FILE OPENING AND DRAG AND DROP
A local SquashFS module opened from the file manager is inspected only. It is
not activated and is not added to Next Boot.
.PP
Drag and drop is an input shortcut. One module opens inspection, Debian package
files populate Packages, a directory populates Folder, and another regular file
populates Installation Script. Dropping an item never executes code or changes
module composition by itself.
.SH MIME TYPE
The application declares support for
.BR application/x-sb .
The MIME type and its *.sb filename association are defined and installed by
MiniOS Tools; Module Manager does not install a competing MIME database entry.
.SH PRIVILEGES
The graphical application is never run as root. Operations that require
privilege are started through pkexec and the relevant MiniOS Tools backend.
.SH REQUIREMENTS
The shared interface requires python3-minios-gui 1.4.0 or newer. Backend module
management and creation require minios-tools 1.7.0 or newer.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sb (1),
.BR dir2sb (1),
.BR sb2dir (1),
.BR apt2sb (1),
.BR script2sb (1),
.BR chroot2sb (1),
.BR savechanges (1)
